## Summary

Replace the util-linux `script`/external `timeout`/shell `stty` terminal-lifecycle harness with a kernel-backed Linux PTY controlled directly from Rust.

The new harness:

- creates a PTY with `openpty(3)`;
- makes the child a session leader and attaches the slave as controlling terminal;
- writes test input through the PTY master;
- drains terminal output without shell wrappers;
- enforces timeout in Rust;
- captures `termios` before/after to verify exact terminal restoration;
- retains the existing real-process persistence, panic, CLI↔TUI, Shift+C, quit and detach assertions.

This is test infrastructure only; no runtime/product behavior changes.

## Motivation

Fedora 44's util-linux `script` behavior caused five terminal lifecycle tests to fail despite the same application behavior being certified elsewhere. Terminal safety tests should depend on kernel PTY semantics, not a distro-specific command wrapper.

## Certification target

- GitHub CI (`ubuntu-latest`): full `cargo test --all-features` with all PTY tests green.
- Fedora 44: the same PTY suite must be fully green; the previous five-failure exception is retired.
- fmt/clippy/full suite/release build remain green.

The Cargo lockfile may receive the expected mechanical addition of the already-locked `libc` crate as a direct dev dependency during native certification.

Presentation-only refinement of the public Dominion OS demo.

